1
00:00:07,307 --> 00:00:11,428
[SPEAKER_01]: There's another Ajahn Chah talk, some excerpts.

2
00:00:12,988 --> 00:00:14,949
[SPEAKER_01]: This talk is called Understanding Dukkha.

3
00:00:19,249 --> 00:00:25,471
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a difficulty in practice, but in anything we undertake, we have to pass through difficulty to reach ease.

4
00:00:26,591 --> 00:00:33,092
[SPEAKER_01]: In dhamma practice, we begin with the truth of dukkha, the pervasive unsatisfactoriness of existence.

5
00:00:33,932 --> 00:00:37,053
[SPEAKER_01]: But as soon as we experience this, we lose heart.

6
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:39,341
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't want to look at it.

7
00:00:40,921 --> 00:00:43,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha is really the truth.

8
00:00:43,843 --> 00:00:45,744
[SPEAKER_01]: But we want to get around it somehow.

9
00:00:46,184 --> 00:00:51,307
[SPEAKER_01]: It's similar to the way we don't like to look at old people, but prefer to look at those who are young.

10
00:00:54,028 --> 00:00:59,491
[SPEAKER_01]: If we don't want to look at dukkha, we will never understand dukkha, no matter how many births we go through.

11
00:01:00,772 --> 00:01:01,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha is a noble truth.

12
00:01:02,853 --> 00:01:07,215
[SPEAKER_01]: If we allow ourselves to face it, then we will start to seek a way out of it.

13
00:01:09,211 --> 00:01:12,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Opposing our habits creates some suffering.

14
00:01:12,592 --> 00:01:14,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Generally, we are afraid of suffering.

15
00:01:16,013 --> 00:01:20,034
[SPEAKER_01]: If something will make us suffer, we don't want to do it.

16
00:01:20,214 --> 00:01:27,697
[SPEAKER_01]: We are interested in what appears to be good and beautiful, but we feel that anything involving suffering is bad.

17
00:01:27,737 --> 00:01:28,497
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like that.

18
00:01:29,117 --> 00:01:31,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is a noble truth.

19
00:01:33,418 --> 00:01:35,779
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is suffering in the heart, it

20
00:01:36,352 --> 00:01:39,853
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... it becomes the cause that makes you think about escaping.

21
00:01:41,053 --> 00:01:42,334
[SPEAKER_01]: It leads you to contemplate.

22
00:01:43,254 --> 00:01:51,317
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't sleep so soundly, because you will be intent on investigating, to find out what is really going on, trying to see the causes and the results.

23
00:01:53,197 --> 00:02:01,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first thing he's saying there is, eh... the first thing you must do, in order to free yourself from suffering, is suffer.

24
00:02:04,095 --> 00:02:16,724
[SPEAKER_00]: If your attitude is all about avoiding suffering, like the non-transparency we discussed before, then you can't free yourself from suffering, because your only concern is to not acknowledge the fact that you suffer.

25
00:02:17,784 --> 00:02:22,288
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you also have the other way of suffering, but then expecting your

26
00:02:26,445 --> 00:02:35,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Expecting things you do, things you observe, environment, circumstances, your livelihood, you expect those things to sort out your suffering.

27
00:02:37,373 --> 00:02:53,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to suffer, you need to recognize the, well, inevitability of suffering, but then you are the one who must understand that, who must make the effort to understand the nature of it, because that's the only freedom from suffering.

28
00:02:54,730 --> 00:03:07,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like you just miraculously get cured from suffering if you repent long enough, or if you endure the physical suffering, mental suffering long enough, everything falls into place.

29
00:03:07,615 --> 00:03:07,775
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

30
00:03:09,395 --> 00:03:14,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Enduring this suffering that arises on account of restraint and so on, yeah, that is the first step.

31
00:03:15,698 --> 00:03:18,319
[SPEAKER_00]: But that in itself will not result in cessation of suffering.

32
00:03:18,839 --> 00:03:21,460
[SPEAKER_00]: If that were so, that's what the Buddha would have said so.

33
00:03:22,155 --> 00:03:25,877
[SPEAKER_00]: But no, it's the understanding of the suffering, the arising, the cessation, and so on.

34
00:03:26,497 --> 00:03:29,278
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what frees you from that suffering.

35
00:03:29,318 --> 00:03:31,479
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, eh... that's exactly what Rājan Chah is saying here.

36
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:41,604
[SPEAKER_00]: If... if you are only concerned about avoiding suffering, maintaining attitude that suffering is bad, you are never gonna, eh... well, acknowledge it.

37
00:03:41,724 --> 00:03:45,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Acknowledge it as a... as a... as a necessary basis for your liberation.

38
00:03:46,126 --> 00:03:49,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Even... even if you practice for liberation, or you think you are,

39
00:03:50,155 --> 00:03:53,598
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not practicing correctly because there is no basis of suffering first.

40
00:03:53,718 --> 00:03:55,779
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha said as well.

41
00:03:55,899 --> 00:03:56,380
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you put it?

42
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:59,842
[SPEAKER_00]: All I teach is suffering and freedom from it.

43
00:03:59,882 --> 00:04:03,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize that people first need to learn how to suffer.

44
00:04:04,165 --> 00:04:08,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the most common attitude with suffering is present is, I must avoid this.

45
00:04:08,428 --> 00:04:09,989
[SPEAKER_00]: I must turn away from this.

46
00:04:10,029 --> 00:04:10,890
[SPEAKER_00]: I must get rid of this.

47
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:16,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, okay, I'll endure this because just going through this suffering will miraculously result in the revelation.

48
00:04:24,218 --> 00:04:25,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how to suffer?

49
00:04:25,759 --> 00:04:27,180
[SPEAKER_01]: How to recognize suffering?

50
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:32,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you don't need to go seeking out the suffering, because you're already in the position of suffering.

51
00:04:32,422 --> 00:04:35,564
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is stop distracting yourself from it.

52
00:04:35,924 --> 00:04:38,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop ignoring it.

53
00:04:38,845 --> 00:04:40,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how to suffer?

54
00:04:40,826 --> 00:04:43,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Or how to recognize that you're already within the domain of suffering?

55
00:04:44,228 --> 00:04:50,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you do so by recognizing the fact that, like, your own life, your own health, your own youth,

56
00:04:52,501 --> 00:04:55,982
[SPEAKER_00]: People around you, your own investment, your own house, your own things you have.

57
00:04:56,802 --> 00:04:59,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine it being taken away tonight.

58
00:05:01,184 --> 00:05:01,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Losing it.

59
00:05:02,584 --> 00:05:04,985
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, see, genuinely contemplate that.

60
00:05:05,445 --> 00:05:07,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Allow those thoughts to arise, to manifest themselves.

61
00:05:08,706 --> 00:05:11,127
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, see, are you going to be emotionally perturbed?

62
00:05:11,867 --> 00:05:13,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you going to be affected by it?

63
00:05:14,282 --> 00:05:15,963
[SPEAKER_00]: And that in itself is already suffering.

64
00:05:16,063 --> 00:05:20,405
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, you're not actually suffering in a sense of you lost everything.

65
00:05:20,885 --> 00:05:21,846
[SPEAKER_00]: You just think about it.

66
00:05:22,566 --> 00:05:23,987
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already suffering.

67
00:05:24,027 --> 00:05:28,129
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly how people avoid that suffering, by refusing to think about it.

68
00:05:28,630 --> 00:05:31,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you must not think about it, because it's depressing.

69
00:05:31,771 --> 00:05:38,194
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly why you can't liberate yourself from what you are liable to, because you stop... you're refusing to acknowledge that you are liable to it.

70
00:05:41,036 --> 00:05:43,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, the whole

71
00:05:43,852 --> 00:05:45,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything you're doing is maintaining.

72
00:05:45,553 --> 00:05:48,494
[SPEAKER_01]: You're maintaining yourself, maintaining your family, maintaining your job.

73
00:05:48,594 --> 00:05:49,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, absolutely, yeah.

74
00:05:49,454 --> 00:05:50,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you stop...

75
00:05:50,815 --> 00:05:51,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Maintaining is good.

76
00:05:52,435 --> 00:05:53,516
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, why are you maintaining it?

77
00:05:53,556 --> 00:05:57,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the thought, the notion of not having it, makes you suffer.

78
00:05:57,677 --> 00:05:58,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, so you are...

79
00:05:58,878 --> 00:06:05,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you're doing is trying to deal with suffering, that at the same time, you're denying it's affecting you.

80
00:06:06,941 --> 00:06:07,561
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I have nothing.

81
00:06:07,621 --> 00:06:08,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing bothers me in my life.

82
00:06:08,461 --> 00:06:08,861
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm happy.

83
00:06:09,922 --> 00:06:13,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well, if you don't do anything tomorrow, if there is no burden,

84
00:06:14,348 --> 00:06:20,712
[SPEAKER_00]: So, everything you do is in order to avoid suffering, including denial of suffering.

85
00:06:20,732 --> 00:06:24,255
[SPEAKER_00]: You do it because you already suffer.

86
00:06:24,435 --> 00:06:25,936
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just not drowning in it.

87
00:06:25,956 --> 00:06:26,376
[SPEAKER_00]: You're ready.

88
00:06:44,287 --> 00:06:44,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Drowning.

89
00:06:45,167 --> 00:06:46,287
[SPEAKER_00]: You're already drowning.

90
00:06:46,387 --> 00:06:49,128
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not learning how to swim across to the safe shore.

91
00:06:49,608 --> 00:06:54,509
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you're doing is learning how to basically keep your mouth above the water.

92
00:06:55,049 --> 00:06:57,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can sustain that only for so long.

93
00:06:57,649 --> 00:07:00,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, you will drown.

94
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:08,851
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha kept talking and giving similes of the crossing the flood, crossing the shore, safety of the other shore and so on.

95
00:07:10,372 --> 00:07:11,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is the only safety.

96
00:07:15,513 --> 00:07:19,095
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll continue with the Ajahn Chah talk.

97
00:07:22,017 --> 00:07:24,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Happy people don't develop wisdom.

98
00:07:24,598 --> 00:07:25,339
[SPEAKER_01]: They are asleep.

99
00:07:26,319 --> 00:07:27,780
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a dog that eats its fill.

100
00:07:28,741 --> 00:07:30,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Afterwards, it doesn't want to do anything.

101
00:07:31,182 --> 00:07:32,723
[SPEAKER_01]: It can sleep all day.

102
00:07:32,783 --> 00:07:34,744
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't bark if a burglar comes.

103
00:07:34,764 --> 00:07:36,205
[SPEAKER_01]: It's too full, too tired.

104
00:07:37,285 --> 00:07:40,507
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you only give it a little food, it'll be alert and await.

105
00:07:41,468 --> 00:07:45,070
[SPEAKER_01]: If someone tries to come sneaking around, it'll jump up and start barking.

106
00:07:46,447 --> 00:07:50,350
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like what we spoke the other day, like the Spartans, the ancient Spartans.

107
00:07:50,370 --> 00:07:51,271
[SPEAKER_01]: The Spartan children.

108
00:07:51,371 --> 00:07:53,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Intentionally, like, underfed.

109
00:07:54,554 --> 00:07:55,534
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're always alert.

110
00:07:56,155 --> 00:07:57,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

111
00:07:59,117 --> 00:08:00,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's that's it.

112
00:08:00,218 --> 00:08:00,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Like happy people.

113
00:08:00,999 --> 00:08:02,220
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly what he means by it.

114
00:08:02,280 --> 00:08:03,060
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what I was saying.

115
00:08:03,601 --> 00:08:07,804
[SPEAKER_00]: People who refuse to acknowledge that even their happiness is under the burden of suffering.

116
00:08:10,666 --> 00:08:13,248
[SPEAKER_00]: By refusing to acknowledge it, you get to enjoy your happiness.

117
00:08:13,368 --> 00:08:14,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Or so it seems.

118
00:08:16,411 --> 00:08:17,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance is bliss.

119
00:08:17,592 --> 00:08:18,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

120
00:08:18,953 --> 00:08:21,395
[SPEAKER_00]: But ignorance is bliss in a sense of...

121
00:08:22,215 --> 00:08:23,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance is never...

122
00:08:23,256 --> 00:08:25,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance is an act of ignoring.

123
00:08:25,978 --> 00:08:31,622
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, as I said before many times, it's not a metaphysical lack of information or mystery.

124
00:08:31,863 --> 00:08:36,326
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually your attitude of ignoring, which means, deep down, you know what you're ignoring.

125
00:08:38,167 --> 00:08:52,792
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why ignorance is undoable, if you make the effort to undo the habit of ignoring certain truths, certain natures of your existence, certain liabilities, certain obviousness of your experience and existence.

126
00:08:54,413 --> 00:08:57,154
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why ignorance and sensuality go hand in hand.

127
00:08:57,654 --> 00:09:01,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality provides you distraction, which helps you...

128
00:09:02,176 --> 00:09:05,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Distraction makes your act of ignoring effortless.

129
00:09:06,224 --> 00:09:08,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Because distracts and pleasure is at the end of this distraction.

130
00:09:10,346 --> 00:09:16,810
[SPEAKER_00]: So, me making the effort to ignore what I deep down inside already know, it becomes much easier.

131
00:09:18,050 --> 00:09:32,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's ignorance, procrastination, all these things, it's sort of like perpetuating that same attitude of fundamental avidya, of not acknowledging certain truths, not refusing to look at it, pretending it's not there, or

132
00:09:34,117 --> 00:09:36,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, you can't pretend it's not there, but then you...

133
00:09:36,358 --> 00:09:37,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I'll deal with it later.

134
00:09:38,078 --> 00:09:38,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, everybody dies.

135
00:09:38,898 --> 00:09:40,999
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll deal with it when my time comes, and so on.

136
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:45,160
[SPEAKER_00]: But how do you think?

137
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:46,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, even if you stop and reflect.

138
00:09:47,241 --> 00:09:47,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Wait a minute.

139
00:09:47,681 --> 00:09:49,242
[SPEAKER_00]: My whole life is about avoiding this.

140
00:09:50,042 --> 00:09:56,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here I am, holding a magical belief that suddenly, when it comes after 50, 60 years on this earth, I'll be able to deal with it.

141
00:09:56,204 --> 00:10:00,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, while I could deal with it, I was refusing to deal with it, because it was too unpleasant.

142
00:10:01,215 --> 00:10:08,299
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's too unpleasant to think about death when you're alive, and somehow, you maintain a notion that you'll be able to deal with it when it's actually occurring?

143
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:15,323
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's unpleasant to think about it, you can only imagine how unpleasant it's going to be when it's actually there.

144
00:10:18,145 --> 00:10:24,428
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll continue with Ajahn Chah.

145
00:10:25,449 --> 00:10:28,671
[SPEAKER_01]: If we speak the subtle dhamma, most people will be frightened by it.

146
00:10:29,251 --> 00:10:30,432
[SPEAKER_01]: They won't dare to enter it.

147
00:10:31,661 --> 00:10:34,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Even saying, don't do evil, most people can't follow.

148
00:10:35,302 --> 00:10:37,243
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how it is.

149
00:10:37,323 --> 00:10:40,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I've sought all kinds of means to get this across.

150
00:10:40,685 --> 00:10:49,588
[SPEAKER_01]: One thing I often say is, no matter if we are delighted or upset, happy or suffering, shedding tears or singing songs, never mind.

151
00:10:50,588 --> 00:10:52,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Living in this world, we are in a cage.

152
00:10:53,429 --> 00:10:55,910
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't get beyond this condition of being in a cage.

153
00:10:56,811 --> 00:10:59,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you are rich, you are living in a cage.

154
00:11:00,714 --> 00:11:02,375
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are poor, you're living in a cage.

155
00:11:02,415 --> 00:11:05,518
[SPEAKER_00]: You're burdened by the same liability to suffering.

156
00:11:05,578 --> 00:11:08,139
[SPEAKER_01]: If you watch a movie, you're watching it in a cage.

157
00:11:08,159 --> 00:11:09,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's exactly what I'm saying.

158
00:11:10,001 --> 00:11:17,906
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to realize that your happiness, watching a movie, family, successful business, is within the burden of suffering.

159
00:11:17,946 --> 00:11:19,047
[SPEAKER_00]: You are caged by it.

160
00:11:20,108 --> 00:11:20,868
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly that.

161
00:11:22,109 --> 00:11:24,291
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like, oh, there's no happiness, everything is depressing.

162
00:11:24,331 --> 00:11:26,372
[SPEAKER_00]: No, there is, but you have to look at the bigger picture.

163
00:11:26,933 --> 00:11:30,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Happiness or grief, it's within the burden of suffering.

164
00:11:30,811 --> 00:11:33,493
[SPEAKER_00]: It's within the liability of suffering.

165
00:11:33,533 --> 00:11:34,454
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the problem.

166
00:11:37,356 --> 00:11:47,784
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you maintain the attitude of ignoring that problem, through taking that happiness for granted, which then means you will be taking avoiding of any grief and suffering for granted as well.

167
00:11:54,269 --> 00:11:55,770
[SPEAKER_01]: What, what is this cage?

168
00:11:56,211 --> 00:11:59,113
[SPEAKER_01]: It is the cage of birth, aging, illness, death.

169
00:12:00,063 --> 00:12:01,863
[SPEAKER_01]: In this way, we are imprisoned in the world.

170
00:12:02,403 --> 00:12:03,004
[SPEAKER_01]: This is mind.

171
00:12:03,484 --> 00:12:04,004
[SPEAKER_01]: This is mind.

172
00:12:04,544 --> 00:12:05,364
[SPEAKER_01]: That belongs to me.

173
00:12:06,224 --> 00:12:09,525
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't know what we really are or what we are doing.

174
00:12:09,725 --> 00:12:10,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, we are all doing.

175
00:12:11,845 --> 00:12:14,986
[SPEAKER_01]: All we are doing is accumulating suffering for ourselves.

176
00:12:15,886 --> 00:12:20,787
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something far away that causes our suffering, but we don't look at ourselves.

177
00:12:20,887 --> 00:12:25,688
[SPEAKER_01]: However much happiness and comfort we may have, having been born, we cannot avoid aging.

178
00:12:26,028 --> 00:12:28,308
[SPEAKER_01]: We must fall ill and we must die.

179
00:12:28,908 --> 00:12:30,968
[SPEAKER_01]: This is dukkha itself, here and now.

180
00:12:31,088 --> 00:12:32,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Hm.

181
00:12:32,609 --> 00:12:35,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Liability of your existence.

182
00:12:37,650 --> 00:12:39,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Not... there's absolutely no alternative to that.

183
00:12:39,570 --> 00:12:41,890
[SPEAKER_00]: The only alternative is to free yourself from it or to ignore it.

184
00:12:47,271 --> 00:12:51,712
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is some excerpts from a Ajahn Chah dhamma talk.

185
00:12:52,972 --> 00:12:55,753
[SPEAKER_01]: It's called, It Can Be Done.

186
00:13:01,316 --> 00:13:03,516
[SPEAKER_01]: This is about halfway through the talk.

187
00:13:06,537 --> 00:13:08,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Nibbāna pācchayo bhūtu.

188
00:13:10,558 --> 00:13:13,919
[SPEAKER_01]: One need not aim at anything or wish for anything at all.

189
00:13:14,459 --> 00:13:16,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Just aim for nibbāna.

190
00:13:18,259 --> 00:13:24,841
[SPEAKER_01]: All manner of becoming and birth, merit and virtue in the worldly way, do not reach there.

191
00:13:26,221 --> 00:13:31,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Making merit and skillful karma, hoping it will cause us to attain to some better state,

192
00:13:32,835 --> 00:13:34,896
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't need to be wishing for a lot of things.

193
00:13:35,336 --> 00:13:37,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Just aim directly for nibbāna.

194
00:13:38,637 --> 00:13:43,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting sīla, wanting tranquility, we just end up in the same old place.

195
00:13:44,979 --> 00:13:47,100
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not necessary to desire these things.

196
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:53,042
[SPEAKER_00]: We should just wish for the place of cessation.

197
00:13:54,603 --> 00:14:00,545
[SPEAKER_01]: The most important point is that while we have life, we should train the mind to be even in regard to things.

198
00:14:01,506 --> 00:14:03,327
[SPEAKER_01]: We should be able to share wealth and possessions.

199
00:14:04,268 --> 00:14:07,109
[SPEAKER_01]: When the time comes, we should give a portion to those in need.

200
00:14:07,950 --> 00:14:10,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as if we were giving things to our own children.

201
00:14:10,271 --> 00:14:14,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Sharing things like this, we will feel happy and we can give away all our wealth.

202
00:14:14,934 --> 00:14:20,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, whenever our breath may stop, there will be no attachment or anxiety, because everything is gone.

203
00:14:22,179 --> 00:14:24,020
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha taught to die before you die.

204
00:14:24,740 --> 00:14:28,002
[SPEAKER_01]: To be finished with things before they are finished.

205
00:14:28,122 --> 00:14:29,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can be at ease.

206
00:14:30,787 --> 00:14:32,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Let things break before they are broken.

207
00:14:33,288 --> 00:14:35,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Let them finish before they are finished.

208
00:14:39,129 --> 00:14:41,269
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the Buddha's intention in teaching the dhamma.

209
00:14:42,289 --> 00:14:51,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you listen to the teachings for a hundred or a thousand eons, if you do not understand these points, you won't be able to undo your suffering and you will not find peace.

210
00:14:52,712 --> 00:14:53,872
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not see the dhamma.

211
00:14:54,652 --> 00:14:58,673
[SPEAKER_01]: But understanding these things according to the Buddha's intention and being able to resolve

212
00:14:59,913 --> 00:15:01,754
[SPEAKER_01]: This view of things is called Sīma Dhamma.

213
00:15:03,114 --> 00:15:06,075
[SPEAKER_01]: This view of things can make an end of suffering.

214
00:15:06,415 --> 00:15:08,496
[SPEAKER_01]: It can relieve all heat and distress.

215
00:15:10,016 --> 00:15:20,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Whoever strives sincerely and is diligent in practice, who can endure, who trains and develops himself to the full measure, those persons will attain to peace and cessation.

216
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:24,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Wherever they stay, they will have no suffering.

217
00:15:24,801 --> 00:15:28,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether they are young or old, they will be free of suffering.

218
00:15:29,046 --> 00:15:38,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever the situation, whatever work they have to perform, they will have no suffering, because their minds have reached the place where suffering is exhausted, where there is peace.

219
00:15:43,557 --> 00:15:50,722
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... the teaching is simply aim at nibbāna.

220
00:15:50,762 --> 00:15:51,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Go for it.

221
00:15:52,984 --> 00:15:53,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

222
00:15:53,585 --> 00:15:55,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to understand what that is.

223
00:15:55,106 --> 00:15:55,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

224
00:15:56,407 --> 00:15:56,767
[SPEAKER_00]: You know that

225
00:15:57,410 --> 00:15:59,592
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to aim at something, you need to know what it is.

226
00:16:00,392 --> 00:16:03,134
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was gonna say, but I decided to let you finish.

227
00:16:03,835 --> 00:16:12,701
[SPEAKER_00]: That bit where it says, like, you want it, means you must know at least where to look for it.

228
00:16:13,762 --> 00:16:15,063
[SPEAKER_00]: You must have some understanding.

229
00:16:15,443 --> 00:16:19,386
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a problem for most people, especially when they start the practice.

230
00:16:20,087 --> 00:16:27,052
[SPEAKER_00]: You kind of... Well, you start with, like, non-understanding of what nibbāna is, of course, but you continue

231
00:16:28,507 --> 00:16:32,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you start making efforts, but none of those efforts are actually aimed at clarifying.

232
00:16:32,409 --> 00:16:35,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, hold on, let me get the idea at least what nibbāna is.

233
00:16:36,332 --> 00:16:39,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Because through getting the idea, you get to know what it is and what you can do about it.

234
00:16:40,915 --> 00:16:49,200
[SPEAKER_00]: But for most people, they expect nibbāna to happen on account of, you know, their commitment to the meditation technique or something.

235
00:16:49,880 --> 00:16:55,003
[SPEAKER_01]: They expect nibbāna to occur and then all my problems will be will be gone.

236
00:16:55,664 --> 00:16:57,645
[SPEAKER_00]: But nibbāna is about undoing things.

237
00:16:58,325 --> 00:17:01,327
[SPEAKER_00]: They make you liable to problems, they make you liable to suffering.

238
00:17:02,747 --> 00:17:08,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbāna is not just a replacement, just a new novelty experience that replaces everything, overrides everything else.

239
00:17:08,830 --> 00:17:23,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbāna is the experience in a sense of when you undone, when you have undone all the unwholesome, all your desire, all your ill-will, all your attitude of distraction and delusion,

240
00:17:24,893 --> 00:17:30,055
[SPEAKER_00]: That negative experience that's left as a result of that undoing, that's what nibbāna is.

241
00:17:30,075 --> 00:17:34,196
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when nibbāna is also a dhamma, a phenomena, but it's asankhata dhamma.

242
00:17:34,516 --> 00:17:37,037
[SPEAKER_00]: It's undetermined by any greed aversion or delusion.

243
00:17:39,077 --> 00:17:53,962
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people will be better off when they start practicing to think... to spend the time thinking about dhamma, than practicing their mindfulness of... sorry, think about dhamma and nibbāna, than practice their mindfulness of breathing or something, which is going to be some mechanical physical technique.

244
00:17:55,375 --> 00:17:56,095
[SPEAKER_00]: You reflect on...

245
00:17:56,336 --> 00:17:58,377
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is... what does the Buddha say nibbāna is?

246
00:17:58,437 --> 00:17:59,277
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a cessation.

247
00:18:00,218 --> 00:18:07,381
[SPEAKER_00]: And... from a point of view of somebody who started the practice, what is the other thing that you could say it's a cessation?

248
00:18:07,602 --> 00:18:08,062
[SPEAKER_00]: It's death.

249
00:18:09,142 --> 00:18:15,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is nibbāna closer, based on the Buddha's description, to what you have the idea that death is?

250
00:18:15,686 --> 00:18:20,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it closer to some kind of transcendental astral projection realm of your liberation?

251
00:18:21,729 --> 00:18:23,490
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, it's closer to death, actually.

252
00:18:25,073 --> 00:18:27,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Because nibbāna is against the grain of life.

253
00:18:27,616 --> 00:18:29,498
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the other thing that's against the grain of life?

254
00:18:29,658 --> 00:18:30,098
[SPEAKER_00]: It's death.

255
00:18:30,979 --> 00:18:36,686
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why people would freak out in the suttas, when they would understand nibbāna, when the Buddha would teach.

256
00:18:36,846 --> 00:18:39,469
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would say, oh, well, that will kill me, that will destroy me.

257
00:18:39,489 --> 00:18:41,971
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would despair and start pulling their hair and so on.

258
00:18:42,652 --> 00:18:44,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Like full-on existential dread.

259
00:18:45,512 --> 00:18:54,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you realize... and the only reason you fear death, fear nibbāna, is because you refuse to give up your possession of your life.

260
00:18:55,096 --> 00:18:55,416
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

261
00:18:55,576 --> 00:18:59,057
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to give up your life, as in you don't have to go and kill yourself to attain nibbāna.

262
00:18:59,097 --> 00:18:59,717
[SPEAKER_00]: That's nonsense.

263
00:19:00,598 --> 00:19:08,801
[SPEAKER_00]: But you must give away the possession of your life, which for most people equates death, which is why it's so frightening.

264
00:19:09,791 --> 00:19:10,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Die before you die.

265
00:19:10,972 --> 00:19:12,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Ajahn Chah said, yeah.

266
00:19:12,513 --> 00:19:13,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Die before you die.

267
00:19:14,414 --> 00:19:15,795
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what it means.

268
00:19:15,815 --> 00:19:21,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbāna is closer to the death from Putujana's point of view, than anything else is.

269
00:19:21,599 --> 00:19:26,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the Buddha himself was teaching maraṇasati as the quickest, sharpest way for enlightenment.

270
00:19:27,043 --> 00:19:28,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Contemplation of death.

271
00:19:28,825 --> 00:19:30,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's a bit too sharp for some people.

272
00:19:32,207 --> 00:19:33,428
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he mellowed it out a bit.

273
00:19:33,968 --> 00:19:36,050
[SPEAKER_00]: But fundamentally, that's exactly why.

274
00:19:37,147 --> 00:19:41,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it directly results in liberation, if you do it rightly.

275
00:19:41,410 --> 00:19:42,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's right.

276
00:19:42,410 --> 00:19:58,298
[SPEAKER_00]: That's also why, like, it said, which is, you know, I know people mystified, but it's, you know, in the suttas, where it said, like, somebody develops the right view, but they haven't reached the fruits of it yet, they don't do it, they do it at the moment of the death, and so on.

277
00:19:59,139 --> 00:20:03,361
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's almost like a mystical, always, in the moment of the death, you get this light revelation.

278
00:20:04,108 --> 00:20:05,149
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

279
00:20:05,429 --> 00:20:11,913
[SPEAKER_00]: You reach the fruits of your right view, for example, or your arahantship, at the moment of your death, because that's when nibbāna is.

280
00:20:13,154 --> 00:20:14,995
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your right view has already understood it.

281
00:20:15,955 --> 00:20:26,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has been preventing you, maybe you were not as diligent as you should have been, but once you're going through the experience of dying, what you're going to have with yourself there, is only your right view, or wrong view.

282
00:20:27,182 --> 00:20:32,846
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you haven't developed the right view during the life, that's why, when death comes, it's too late to do anything about it.

283
00:20:33,500 --> 00:20:35,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you say, your view is there for you.

284
00:20:36,121 --> 00:20:37,701
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you have there, exactly.

285
00:20:38,101 --> 00:20:46,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when your right view is right there, that's why, oh, he gets his enlightenment or becomes an arahant at the moment of dying, as in when he's dying.

286
00:20:47,063 --> 00:20:50,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what nibbāna is, for a living mind.

287
00:20:50,844 --> 00:20:51,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation.

288
00:20:52,484 --> 00:20:53,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of aggression.

289
00:20:54,024 --> 00:20:57,085
[SPEAKER_00]: But now he's there with the right view, not with a vijja.

290
00:20:58,342 --> 00:20:59,262
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the difference.

291
00:20:59,842 --> 00:21:04,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And it would be different for him, because he made the effort while he was still alive.

292
00:21:04,783 --> 00:21:06,104
[SPEAKER_00]: He died before he died.

293
00:21:07,784 --> 00:21:24,048
[SPEAKER_00]: You... you fully remove any possessiveness, any... like, make sure everything ceases, in the sense of not you go around destroying things, but you destroy your emotional dependence upon things, including your own life, your own health, your own youth, your own family, everything, everything, everything.

294
00:21:24,068 --> 00:21:24,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

295
00:21:24,308 --> 00:21:24,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Because

296
00:21:25,108 --> 00:21:26,549
[SPEAKER_00]: You emulate death.

297
00:21:27,149 --> 00:21:31,071
[SPEAKER_00]: When death comes, I can't even conceive a second longer holding on to these things.

298
00:21:32,152 --> 00:21:34,854
[SPEAKER_00]: So we also must know, you don't have to destroy the things.

299
00:21:35,534 --> 00:21:40,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Just fully develop that theme of being unable to hold on to this, if it decides to go away.

300
00:21:43,178 --> 00:21:51,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if destruction of things, if throwing away your possessions, frees you from possessions, then, as I said, all you have to do is take away this life to get nibbāna.

301
00:21:51,603 --> 00:21:52,964
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't work like that, doesn't it?

302
00:21:54,012 --> 00:21:54,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

303
00:21:54,913 --> 00:21:57,094
[SPEAKER_00]: You need this life in order to understand nibbāna.

304
00:21:58,054 --> 00:22:01,376
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might be throwing away your possessions to simplify your life to an extent.

305
00:22:01,696 --> 00:22:07,340
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you think that throwing away your possessions is bringing you closer to nibbāna, well, you're seriously deluded.

306
00:22:07,780 --> 00:22:08,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Still.

307
00:22:09,481 --> 00:22:13,783
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize possessions are not... your possession is not in things.

308
00:22:14,364 --> 00:22:15,825
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your attitude towards the things.

309
00:22:16,885 --> 00:22:18,186
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's something you can't throw away.

310
00:22:21,380 --> 00:22:24,881
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the ultimate possession of the views, and the wrong views, or the right views.

311
00:22:25,702 --> 00:22:27,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Your possession of the wrong views.

312
00:22:27,683 --> 00:22:28,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

313
00:22:28,663 --> 00:22:29,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

314
00:22:29,523 --> 00:22:40,108
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will die before you die, means you make sure you have removed any appropriation, any sense of ownership in regard to your experience as a whole, whatever the content of that experience is.

315
00:22:40,768 --> 00:22:44,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when the experience of death comes, well, there's nothing there that contradicts it.

316
00:22:45,258 --> 00:22:52,483
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no assumption of possession, appropriation, control, that will be directly at odds with the experience of death, which denies it.

317
00:22:53,484 --> 00:22:55,525
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're at peace, even in death.

318
00:22:56,706 --> 00:22:57,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, it's not your death.

319
00:22:57,847 --> 00:23:01,009
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not being one destroyed by that death, because you're not there to be destroyed.

320
00:23:01,169 --> 00:23:04,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like what Ajahn Chah says, be finished before it's finished.

321
00:23:04,632 --> 00:23:07,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, sure, sure, exactly, yeah, yeah.

322
00:23:07,454 --> 00:23:12,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Finish the thing before they are finished, because they are going to be finished, for sure.

323
00:23:12,461 --> 00:23:16,463
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a... for an arahant who is finished, who is in nibbāna, death is parinibbāna.

324
00:23:16,503 --> 00:23:17,664
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not even death.

325
00:23:17,684 --> 00:23:20,025
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha wouldn't even use the word death for an arahant.

326
00:23:20,125 --> 00:23:21,666
[SPEAKER_00]: He would say the aggregates break apart.

327
00:23:22,607 --> 00:23:24,227
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't use the same Pali word for it.

328
00:23:24,868 --> 00:23:25,828
[SPEAKER_00]: But for Putujanā, yeah.

329
00:23:26,209 --> 00:23:26,689
[SPEAKER_00]: He dies.

330
00:23:27,629 --> 00:23:31,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, you don't die because your aggregates break apart.

331
00:23:31,952 --> 00:23:35,434
[SPEAKER_00]: You die because the aggregates break apart that you are appropriating them.

332
00:23:35,874 --> 00:23:36,614
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you die.

333
00:23:37,094 --> 00:23:38,855
[SPEAKER_00]: You're appropriating them and they break apart.

334
00:23:39,236 --> 00:23:39,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is death.

335
00:23:41,237 --> 00:23:41,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's

336
00:23:42,245 --> 00:23:50,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, arahant cannot prevent five aggregates from breaking apart, cannot prevent body from withering away and dying.

337
00:23:51,932 --> 00:23:58,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if that's truly what death is, as in the mere destruction of the body, then nobody can be free from that.

338
00:23:58,237 --> 00:23:58,757
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

339
00:23:59,338 --> 00:24:07,824
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the appropriation of that which will be destructed, destroyed by the death or accident or old age or illness, sickness.

340
00:24:10,193 --> 00:24:14,015
[SPEAKER_00]: That appropriation makes it death, aging and sickness and so on.

341
00:24:14,635 --> 00:24:15,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Without it, it isn't.

342
00:24:17,357 --> 00:24:18,758
It's just a falling apart.

343
00:24:18,878 --> 00:24:20,779
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... you can designate whichever way you want.

344
00:24:21,159 --> 00:24:22,080
[SPEAKER_01]: My cup has died.

345
00:24:22,420 --> 00:24:22,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

346
00:24:22,880 --> 00:24:26,822
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just basically a change, cessation of this, something else.

347
00:24:26,902 --> 00:24:33,746
[SPEAKER_00]: But if there is no appropriation, means none of it will be yours, which means you are done in regard to the five aggregates.

348
00:24:35,449 --> 00:24:49,275
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is, this is obviously much subtler thing, harder thing to see, and it's much easier to just either deny your life, deny your possessions, throw everything away, burn your house, must not own anything, must not help anyone, because everything is attachment.

349
00:24:50,395 --> 00:24:53,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind is the attachment, not things you get attached to.

350
00:24:55,157 --> 00:25:02,340
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you change your mind of being... from being mind of attachment to mind of non-attachment, everything else can stay the same.

351
00:25:03,340 --> 00:25:04,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Except, of course, the basic virtue.

352
00:25:08,578 --> 00:25:09,539
[SPEAKER_00]: That needs to be done.

353
00:25:09,659 --> 00:25:15,802
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, certain amount of engagement with the world, in an unwholesome sense, must be restrained.

354
00:25:16,603 --> 00:25:18,123
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, everything else...

355
00:25:18,944 --> 00:25:21,806
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the Buddha himself would have stayed in the king's palaces and whatnot.

356
00:25:22,806 --> 00:25:26,368
[SPEAKER_00]: And if that's how you measure the attachment, it means he was extremely attached.

357
00:25:26,608 --> 00:25:27,829
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's a ridiculous thing to say.

358
00:25:30,631 --> 00:25:32,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that doesn't mean, also, I can go and stay in the palaces.

359
00:25:32,552 --> 00:25:33,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, are you the Buddha?

360
00:25:34,193 --> 00:25:34,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you an Arahant?

361
00:25:35,343 --> 00:25:36,624
[SPEAKER_00]: If not, well, you have to be careful.

362
00:25:37,124 --> 00:25:42,328
[SPEAKER_00]: You're careful, in a sense, you have to see what is it that makes it wholesome or unwholesome?

363
00:25:42,368 --> 00:25:46,791
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it that makes it, it doesn't matter if I stay in King's Palace, or what is it that matters?

364
00:25:47,312 --> 00:25:49,773
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, I'm becoming attached to staying in King's Palace.

365
00:25:50,634 --> 00:25:51,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you aware of that?

366
00:25:51,615 --> 00:25:54,317
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are, then, yeah, you probably won't be affected wherever you stay.

367
00:25:54,997 --> 00:25:56,618
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be at ease, as Ajahn Chah says.

368
00:25:57,019 --> 00:25:59,460
[SPEAKER_00]: By things coming, going, having, losing, doesn't matter.

369
00:26:00,221 --> 00:26:01,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, your mind becomes even.

370
00:26:01,682 --> 00:26:03,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, even, yeah.

371
00:26:07,602 --> 00:26:14,906
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nibbāna being non-greed, non-hatred, non-delusion.

372
00:26:16,567 --> 00:26:17,648
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we can develop this.

373
00:26:18,828 --> 00:26:19,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

374
00:26:19,569 --> 00:26:19,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Every day.

375
00:26:20,409 --> 00:26:20,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Every day.

376
00:26:21,570 --> 00:26:31,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Through clarifying what greed, what desire for sensual pleasures and so on is, you get to discern what non-greed is.

377
00:26:32,516 --> 00:26:36,278
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I'm greedy for something,

378
00:26:37,583 --> 00:26:40,846
[SPEAKER_01]: I can acknowledge that, and not engage in it.

379
00:26:41,147 --> 00:26:41,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

380
00:26:41,667 --> 00:26:43,369
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, your body needs to be restrained.

381
00:26:43,969 --> 00:26:50,155
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not habitually just giving in to it, even before you recognize the thought being present, intention towards those things present.

382
00:26:51,156 --> 00:27:00,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you're physically restrained, then, yeah, you can start noticing it on the level of your mind, and then you realize, okay, these thoughts have arisen on their own, but I'm not obliged to say yes to the desire that has arisen.

383
00:27:01,502 --> 00:27:05,227
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to try and get rid of it, because it wasn't my doing in the first place.

384
00:27:05,267 --> 00:27:07,289
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't create this desire in my mind.

385
00:27:07,329 --> 00:27:07,910
[SPEAKER_00]: It has arisen.

386
00:27:08,010 --> 00:27:08,771
[SPEAKER_00]: It manifested.

387
00:27:10,052 --> 00:27:11,835
[SPEAKER_00]: But I can certainly...

388
00:27:11,855 --> 00:27:17,662
[SPEAKER_00]: I am certainly responsible if I choose to welcome it, if I choose to entertain it, if I choose to think further upon it.

389
00:27:18,222 --> 00:27:18,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with ill-will.

390
00:27:19,284 --> 00:27:20,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with distraction.

391
00:27:21,306 --> 00:27:21,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

392
00:27:22,566 --> 00:27:31,368
[SPEAKER_00]: It's better of the... rather than, like, sitting and trying to get experience from meditation, sit quietly and make a meditation about experience of understanding what these things are.

393
00:27:31,928 --> 00:27:33,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where the peace is.

394
00:27:33,748 --> 00:27:43,730
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're not affected by your thoughts changing, when you're not affected by the desire arising, or disagreeable displeasure arising, or boredom arising.

395
00:27:43,810 --> 00:27:46,391
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't affect you, means you don't have to act out of it.

396
00:27:46,927 --> 00:27:54,370
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't act out of it, you stop perpetuating the very things that prevent you from experiencing nibbāna, greed, aversion, delusion.

397
00:27:57,672 --> 00:28:06,536
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, if you do that for one hour... Yeah, you do that for one hour, but it wasn't the hour of kind of mindless sort of head nodding...

398
00:28:07,773 --> 00:28:08,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Flawed meditation.

399
00:28:08,534 --> 00:28:09,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Flawed meditation.

400
00:28:09,354 --> 00:28:14,799
[SPEAKER_00]: As we said before, it's an hour of clarifying the meaning of these mental states.

401
00:28:14,919 --> 00:28:21,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, even when you're not in your meditation then, when you go about your life, at the back of your mind, those themes are still going to be present.

402
00:28:22,446 --> 00:28:25,208
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you start to meditate, regardless of your posture.

403
00:28:25,949 --> 00:28:27,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not I'm meditating, I'm meditating.

404
00:28:27,490 --> 00:28:29,532
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just develop the theme, establish your mind.

405
00:28:30,252 --> 00:28:34,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Upon the perception of the non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion, perception of renunciation.

406
00:28:34,976 --> 00:28:35,297
[SPEAKER_00]: All those

407
00:28:36,162 --> 00:28:44,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Meditations that the Buddha talks about in the suttas are basically themes upon which you can establish your mind if you spend long enough time dwelling on it.

408
00:28:46,089 --> 00:28:47,029
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha would say.

409
00:28:47,310 --> 00:28:52,553
[SPEAKER_00]: His mind turns towards it, inclines, he doesn't turn towards company, because he's been thinking about this.

410
00:28:53,574 --> 00:28:54,915
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what he starts to value.

411
00:28:55,475 --> 00:28:57,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he values it, he establishes himself upon it.

412
00:28:58,477 --> 00:29:01,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, nothing inferior to those states can disturb him.

413
00:29:07,709 --> 00:29:15,579
[SPEAKER_00]: I like it, the theme, the theme of nibbāna, making your life... Oh, nibbāna is just like, wow, it's so... why is it unattainable?

414
00:29:16,721 --> 00:29:18,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Absence of greed, aversion, delusion, that's what nibbāna is.

415
00:29:18,964 --> 00:29:24,270
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just start looking at how much percentage of your daily life

416
00:29:25,067 --> 00:29:35,958
[SPEAKER_00]: You spend cultivating desire, welcoming it, being careless about it, ill-will, distraction, oh, I'm bored, I'm gonna do this, I'm gonna read this, I'm gonna watch that, I'm gonna go and talk to these people.

417
00:29:36,338 --> 00:29:46,789
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize, oh no, if I were to stop doing these things, rooted in desire, rooted in ill-will, rooted in delusion and distraction, that brings me closer to nibbāna.

418
00:29:47,980 --> 00:29:53,043
[SPEAKER_00]: That's closer to what nibbāna is than anything else I do on top of these things.

419
00:29:53,323 --> 00:29:55,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's going become a monk for 60 years and whatnot.

420
00:29:58,347 --> 00:30:02,169
[SPEAKER_00]: If you do that on top of your greed, aversion, delusion, you're not a step closer to nibbāna.

421
00:30:07,912 --> 00:30:15,077
[SPEAKER_01]: And just by doing that, refraining from those unwholesome things, then it doesn't really matter what...

422
00:30:15,996 --> 00:30:18,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Your life will form around it, you know what I mean?

423
00:30:18,798 --> 00:30:18,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

424
00:30:18,958 --> 00:30:20,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you become a monk or not, that's not the point.

425
00:30:20,979 --> 00:30:22,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's not the point, exactly.

426
00:30:22,540 --> 00:30:24,521
[SPEAKER_01]: But things will start to form around your life.

427
00:30:24,561 --> 00:30:27,623
[SPEAKER_01]: You will go hang around monasteries, maybe.

428
00:30:27,843 --> 00:30:28,684
[SPEAKER_01]: You might become a monk.

429
00:30:28,984 --> 00:30:31,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you might not hang around, depending on the monastery, I guess.

430
00:30:32,566 --> 00:30:38,870
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, the point is, your mind will be then inclining towards things that the Buddha was describing as the mind will be inclining to.

431
00:30:39,807 --> 00:30:48,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion, non-company, renunciation, release, finding basically joy in that solitude, in that non-engagement.

432
00:30:48,555 --> 00:30:51,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you're afraid of engagement or you don't know how to deal with it.

433
00:30:51,758 --> 00:30:56,182
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's because you're contemplating the theme of nibbāna which naturally results in this.

434
00:30:57,263 --> 00:30:59,425
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, when engagement comes, it won't bother you.

435
00:30:59,885 --> 00:31:03,589
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will certainly not try to make it stay longer because you're actually bored on your own.

436
00:31:03,709 --> 00:31:04,670
[SPEAKER_00]: No, quite the contrary.

437
00:31:06,773 --> 00:31:13,016
[SPEAKER_00]: You do it as a sense of duty, you have no problem with that, but you won't be doing it a second longer, for any other reason.

438
00:31:14,116 --> 00:31:29,323
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha meant, says, when the... when the... like a monk whose mind is inclined towards nibbāna, seclusion practice, when he's visited by kings and retinues and other people, he would just say enough to answer their questions, but not a word longer to make them kind of linger or stay.

439
00:31:30,484 --> 00:31:32,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Just... yeah, you don't want them there, basically.

440
00:31:32,685 --> 00:31:34,786
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want anyone, anywhere around you.

441
00:31:35,752 --> 00:31:36,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the whole point.

442
00:31:37,393 --> 00:31:42,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion kind of ties in with that whole idea of cessation and renunciation.

443
00:31:46,120 --> 00:31:52,785
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean that automatically, just by locking yourself alone in the room, you're suddenly practicing seclusion of nibbāna.

444
00:31:52,845 --> 00:31:52,985
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

445
00:31:53,445 --> 00:31:58,489
[SPEAKER_00]: It's seclusion of greed... seclusion from your own mind of greed, aversion, delusion.

446
00:31:59,129 --> 00:32:04,073
[SPEAKER_00]: That then gets sort of exemplified with also physical seclusion and all these other things.

447
00:32:04,829 --> 00:32:11,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So, physical seclusion can help you, but don't mistake that to be like this seclusion that results in liberation of your mind.

448
00:32:11,951 --> 00:32:14,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, one thing for sure, company will not help either way.

449
00:32:15,851 --> 00:32:20,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless it's a company of somebody with the right view or higher, but then, in that case, they wouldn't want to have you near them.

450
00:32:22,613 --> 00:32:24,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would they associate with a Bhutujana?

451
00:32:26,134 --> 00:32:29,414
[SPEAKER_01]: You've just got to be honest with yourself.

452
00:32:29,774 --> 00:32:32,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this causing more greed?

453
00:32:33,719 --> 00:32:34,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

454
00:32:34,899 --> 00:32:36,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I going for this now?

455
00:32:36,680 --> 00:32:40,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it because I'm just trying to, like, get away from stuff?

456
00:32:41,281 --> 00:32:43,942
[SPEAKER_00]: What stuff?

457
00:32:43,962 --> 00:32:45,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the stuff I'm trying to get away unwholesome?

458
00:32:45,963 --> 00:32:47,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, if it's unwholesome, it's fine.

459
00:32:47,703 --> 00:32:48,743
[SPEAKER_00]: I just wanted to get away.

460
00:32:49,544 --> 00:32:52,684
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it's not unwholesome, then why am I trying to get away from it?

461
00:32:53,045 --> 00:32:54,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I bothered by it?

462
00:32:54,765 --> 00:32:57,926
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to realize that the responsibility for your own suffering is only on you.

463
00:32:58,817 --> 00:33:02,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means responsibility from liberation of your own suffering will be also on you.

464
00:33:02,961 --> 00:33:05,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Circumstances do not make you suffer.

465
00:33:05,743 --> 00:33:08,826
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your own attitude, in regard to circumstances, that makes you suffer.

466
00:33:10,308 --> 00:33:15,312
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is another... Well, we're still from that talk, It Can Be Done, Adin Shah.

467
00:33:16,613 --> 00:33:17,454
[SPEAKER_01]: A few more excerpts.

468
00:33:18,055 --> 00:33:19,336
[SPEAKER_01]: This is from the beginning of the talk.

469
00:33:23,319 --> 00:33:31,142
[SPEAKER_01]: When it comes to taking precepts, for example, a monk will proclaim the precepts and the lay people will vow to undertake them.

470
00:33:31,182 --> 00:33:32,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't misunderstand what is going on.

471
00:33:33,783 --> 00:33:37,244
[SPEAKER_01]: The truth is that morality is not something that can be given.

472
00:33:37,284 --> 00:33:39,865
[SPEAKER_01]: It can't really be requested or received from someone.

473
00:33:40,665 --> 00:33:42,025
[SPEAKER_01]: We can't give it to someone else.

474
00:33:43,266 --> 00:33:49,168
[SPEAKER_01]: In our vernacular, we hear people say, the venerable monk gave the precepts and we received the precepts.

475
00:33:50,477 --> 00:33:55,339
[SPEAKER_01]: We talk like this here in the countryside, and so it has become our habitual way of understanding.

476
00:33:56,480 --> 00:34:15,310
[SPEAKER_01]: If we think like like this, that we come to receive precepts from the monks or on the lunar observance days, and that if the monks won't give the precepts, then we don't have morality, that is only a tradition of delusion that that we have inherited from our ancestors.

477
00:34:16,810 --> 00:34:20,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking in this way means that we give up our own responsibility

478
00:34:20,656 --> 00:34:26,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Not having firm trust and conviction in ourselves, then it gets passed down to the next generation.

479
00:34:26,940 --> 00:34:31,383
[SPEAKER_01]: And they too come to receive precepts from the monk... to receive precepts from the monk.

480
00:34:32,404 --> 00:34:36,706
[SPEAKER_01]: And the monks come to believe that they are the ones who give the precepts to the laity.

481
00:34:37,927 --> 00:34:41,429
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, morality and precepts are not like that.

482
00:34:41,449 --> 00:34:46,613
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not something to be given or received.

483
00:34:47,473 --> 00:34:50,295
[SPEAKER_01]: In truth, morality resides with the intentions of people.

484
00:34:51,224 --> 00:35:01,110
[SPEAKER_01]: If we have the conscious determination to refrain from harmful activities and wrongdoing by way of body speech, then morality is coming about within you.

485
00:35:01,970 --> 00:35:03,351
[SPEAKER_01]: You should know it within yourself.

486
00:35:04,652 --> 00:35:07,393
[SPEAKER_01]: It is okay to take the vows with another person.

487
00:35:07,413 --> 00:35:09,435
[SPEAKER_01]: You can also recollect the precepts by yourself.

488
00:35:10,515 --> 00:35:14,377
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know what they are, then you can request them from someone else.

489
00:35:15,458 --> 00:35:17,479
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something very complicated or distant.

490
00:35:18,420 --> 00:35:18,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So really,

491
00:35:19,740 --> 00:35:24,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever we wish to receive morality and dhamma, we have them right here and now.

492
00:35:25,765 --> 00:35:28,108
[SPEAKER_01]: It is just like the air that surrounds us everywhere.

493
00:35:28,688 --> 00:35:30,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever we breathe, we take it in.

494
00:35:30,910 --> 00:35:32,632
[SPEAKER_01]: All manner of good and evil is like that.

495
00:35:33,472 --> 00:35:37,056
[SPEAKER_01]: If we wish to do good, we can do it anywhere, at any time.

496
00:35:37,856 --> 00:35:40,179
[SPEAKER_01]: We can do it alone or together with others.

497
00:35:40,359 --> 00:35:41,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Evil is the same.

498
00:35:42,381 --> 00:35:45,964
[SPEAKER_01]: We can do it with a large or small group, in a hidden or open place.

499
00:35:49,423 --> 00:35:51,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's kind of self-explanatory, really.

500
00:35:53,045 --> 00:36:04,591
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about, again, it's about don't project responsibility for your own virtue and morality on the external form that you can use to learn from or to remind you of it, but ultimately it's on you.

501
00:36:06,352 --> 00:36:16,198
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, if you haven't had the chance to formally take the precepts in the monastery, it's simply completely unrelatable to whether you keep the precepts or not.

502
00:36:17,148 --> 00:36:18,909
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one making the magical connection.

503
00:36:18,969 --> 00:36:22,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I can't be moral now, because I haven't recited it in front of a monk.

504
00:36:22,090 --> 00:36:22,310
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

505
00:36:23,291 --> 00:36:28,893
[SPEAKER_00]: You recite it to remind yourself what you should be doing, whether you recite or not, which is to keep the precepts.

506
00:36:30,574 --> 00:36:34,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Five precepts is what Buddha said distinguishes humans from animals.

507
00:36:34,716 --> 00:36:43,720
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll continue a little bit there, because what you just said now... Ajahn Chah says that morality, this is something that we should consider normal for all humans to practice.

508
00:36:44,160 --> 00:36:44,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

509
00:36:45,222 --> 00:36:48,564
[SPEAKER_01]: A person who has no morality is no different from an animal.

510
00:36:48,824 --> 00:36:56,348
[SPEAKER_01]: If you decide to live like an animal, then, of course, there is no good or evil for you, because an animal doesn't have any knowledge of such things.

511
00:36:57,248 --> 00:37:03,912
[SPEAKER_01]: A cat catches mice, but we don't say it is doing evil, because it has no concept of knowledge of good or bad, right or wrong.

512
00:37:05,273 --> 00:37:07,654
[SPEAKER_01]: These beings are outside the circle of human beings.

513
00:37:08,154 --> 00:37:09,075
[SPEAKER_01]: It is the animal realm.

514
00:37:10,035 --> 00:37:14,978
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha pointed out that this group is just living according to the animal kind of actions.

515
00:37:15,772 --> 00:37:18,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Those who understand right and wrong, good and evil, are humans.

516
00:37:20,395 --> 00:37:22,617
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha taught this dhamma for humans.

517
00:37:23,538 --> 00:37:30,462
[SPEAKER_01]: If we... if we people don't have morality and knowledge of these things, then we are not much different from animals.

518
00:37:31,223 --> 00:37:37,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is appropriate that... appropriate that we study and learn about them and... and make ourselves able.

519
00:37:38,788 --> 00:37:45,353
[SPEAKER_01]: This is taking advantage of the precious accomplishment of human existence and bringing it to fulfillment.

520
00:37:47,012 --> 00:37:51,234
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as we said just a few minutes ago, it's nothing...

521
00:37:51,254 --> 00:38:00,239
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you might have to do it initially, learn about it, take that kind of formal sense, but you recognize it's about your day-to-day life.

522
00:38:01,219 --> 00:38:05,001
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about what goes on in your mind throughout day-to-day life.

523
00:38:05,021 --> 00:38:06,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just watch your mind.

524
00:38:07,102 --> 00:38:07,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

525
00:38:07,923 --> 00:38:08,383
[SPEAKER_00]: That's good.

526
00:38:09,203 --> 00:38:10,104
[SPEAKER_00]: But watch your mind for what?

527
00:38:12,605 --> 00:38:13,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Watch your mind for unwholesome.

528
00:38:13,926 --> 00:38:14,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

529
00:38:14,286 --> 00:38:15,066
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see unwholesome.

530
00:38:15,106 --> 00:38:15,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you do about it?

531
00:38:16,674 --> 00:38:18,634
[SPEAKER_00]: You try to get rid of it, the level of your mind.

532
00:38:19,214 --> 00:38:21,575
[SPEAKER_00]: But that kind of implies you're in control of your mind.

533
00:38:21,675 --> 00:38:23,775
[SPEAKER_00]: You're in control of what arises in your mind.

534
00:38:23,835 --> 00:38:24,635
[SPEAKER_00]: You have say in it.

535
00:38:25,216 --> 00:38:25,696
[SPEAKER_00]: But do you?

536
00:38:27,096 --> 00:38:30,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Not according to the Buddha, whereby mind and the thoughts is just another sense.

537
00:38:31,517 --> 00:38:32,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind wants to think.

538
00:38:32,897 --> 00:38:33,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Eyes want to see.

539
00:38:34,537 --> 00:38:35,417
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's not on you.

540
00:38:35,997 --> 00:38:37,958
[SPEAKER_00]: So then you realize, okay, so what is on me then?

541
00:38:39,238 --> 00:38:42,579
[SPEAKER_00]: When thoughts of desire or thoughts of ill will present themselves.

542
00:38:43,119 --> 00:38:43,779
[SPEAKER_00]: What is on me?

543
00:38:43,919 --> 00:38:45,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is the line there?

544
00:38:46,245 --> 00:38:51,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Up to which I'm responsible, before or after which I might not be.

545
00:38:53,991 --> 00:38:57,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all these reflections is something people don't usually do.

546
00:38:57,454 --> 00:39:05,780
[SPEAKER_00]: You just want to be told what to do, told where to focus, told what to read, if anything, and things will fall into place.

547
00:39:06,140 --> 00:39:06,280
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

548
00:39:06,621 --> 00:39:07,701
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no falling into place.

549
00:39:07,922 --> 00:39:10,844
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you building a place and then putting things there.

550
00:39:12,165 --> 00:39:12,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally.

551
00:39:13,466 --> 00:39:14,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Manly effort, manly strength,

552
00:39:15,405 --> 00:39:18,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Manly determination, as the suttas would often describe it.

553
00:39:19,508 --> 00:39:25,672
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you ask yourself, throughout your day life, okay, so... Is my mind affected by desire now?

554
00:39:25,712 --> 00:39:29,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Are there things that I'm... maybe I don't want right now, but I might think about something I want later.

555
00:39:30,816 --> 00:39:31,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I want it?

556
00:39:31,757 --> 00:39:32,157
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

557
00:39:32,297 --> 00:39:34,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it for my benefit, for benefit of others?

558
00:39:34,619 --> 00:39:35,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Whose benefit?

559
00:39:35,839 --> 00:39:36,640
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the result of it?

560
00:39:36,720 --> 00:39:40,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it genuine benefit to to my livelihood, to my health?

561
00:39:41,463 --> 00:39:43,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it just a distraction and pleasure?

562
00:39:44,845 --> 00:39:46,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, sometimes it can be mixed.

563
00:39:46,106 --> 00:39:46,726
[SPEAKER_00]: You look further.

564
00:39:46,786 --> 00:39:51,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so it's for my health, but it also... Are there alternatives for my health that will not necessarily be as pleasant?

565
00:39:52,329 --> 00:39:52,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, there are.

566
00:39:54,391 --> 00:39:59,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Would I be affected if, say, I go for those alternatives, but I don't go for the alternatives of pleasure that I want?

567
00:40:01,094 --> 00:40:06,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you ask yourself, will I be emotionally disturbed if I don't get the pleasure I want?

568
00:40:07,338 --> 00:40:09,619
[SPEAKER_00]: If so, you don't go for that pleasure.

569
00:40:10,640 --> 00:40:11,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there and then.

570
00:40:12,701 --> 00:40:14,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Next time, you might not be disturbed.

571
00:40:15,325 --> 00:40:20,169
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have to be honest with yourself and transparent, so that these questions can start to apply.

572
00:40:21,390 --> 00:40:23,952
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why I keep saying, like, don't necessarily rush to give the answer.

573
00:40:24,012 --> 00:40:28,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not about the answer, it's about becoming a question yourself.

574
00:40:28,815 --> 00:40:31,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's how you start to question your own existential attitude.

575
00:40:32,238 --> 00:40:34,440
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like the expression, like, oh, let that sink in.

576
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:36,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, don't rush, just let it sink in.

577
00:40:36,421 --> 00:40:38,143
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly what you're doing.

578
00:40:38,263 --> 00:40:41,305
[SPEAKER_00]: You bring out the theme through a question, but you don't rush answering it.

579
00:40:41,465 --> 00:40:42,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Just let it endure.

580
00:40:43,218 --> 00:40:45,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Even for a day or two, let it be on your mind.

581
00:40:46,460 --> 00:40:48,582
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a theme you're developing for a day or two.

582
00:40:49,323 --> 00:40:50,663
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already a form of samādhi.

583
00:40:51,444 --> 00:40:54,606
[SPEAKER_00]: It's sinking in, that particular thing you're reflecting.

584
00:40:56,047 --> 00:40:56,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

585
00:40:56,388 --> 00:40:59,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I prefer this over that?

586
00:40:59,850 --> 00:41:00,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I fear this?

587
00:41:01,231 --> 00:41:02,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is this a problem?

588
00:41:04,273 --> 00:41:09,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, it all comes down to, am I emotionally affected by these things?

589
00:41:10,580 --> 00:41:14,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I... does this pleasure, emotionally, make me angry?

590
00:41:15,623 --> 00:41:16,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Make me lash out?

591
00:41:17,184 --> 00:41:19,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Does pleasure, emotionally, make me crave for it?

592
00:41:20,106 --> 00:41:21,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Make me go for sensuality?

593
00:41:22,147 --> 00:41:26,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Does neutral feeling, emotionally, make me sort of just turn to oblivion and distraction?

594
00:41:26,950 --> 00:41:28,991
[SPEAKER_00]: If so, you don't cultivate that.

595
00:41:29,511 --> 00:41:32,613
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't mean you try to get rid of everything and stop everything.

596
00:41:32,633 --> 00:41:35,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize responsibility is not for the thoughts that have a reason.

597
00:41:35,655 --> 00:41:37,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Responsibility is for acting out of it,

598
00:41:37,922 --> 00:41:39,543
[SPEAKER_00]: For welcoming it, as I said before.

599
00:41:39,623 --> 00:41:42,025
[SPEAKER_00]: For entertaining it, as the Buddha described so many times.

600
00:41:42,585 --> 00:41:43,505
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you do.

601
00:41:44,186 --> 00:41:46,027
[SPEAKER_00]: But many people would think, okay, so I must not...

602
00:41:46,127 --> 00:41:47,028
[SPEAKER_00]: I must stop thinking this.

603
00:41:47,088 --> 00:41:47,888
[SPEAKER_00]: I must not think this.

604
00:41:48,508 --> 00:41:49,489
[SPEAKER_00]: I have thoughts of sensuality.

605
00:41:49,509 --> 00:41:50,970
[SPEAKER_00]: I must stop thinking that.

606
00:41:50,990 --> 00:41:52,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you must stop indulging that.

607
00:41:52,931 --> 00:41:58,374
[SPEAKER_00]: But you must also stop trying to get rid of it in a sense of the you are the master controller of it in the first place.

608
00:41:58,395 --> 00:42:00,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that becomes your form of engagement with it.

609
00:42:00,756 --> 00:42:01,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Trying to negate it.

610
00:42:02,497 --> 00:42:03,738
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you remain bound to it.

611
00:42:04,838 --> 00:42:07,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it wasn't your doing, the arising of those thoughts

612
00:42:07,925 --> 00:42:17,688
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in order to arrive at the point where you can start seeing those thoughts in your mind like that, you must have... you must develop virtue and, like, restrain your physical actions and your speech.

613
00:42:18,289 --> 00:42:20,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you must toughen up, basically.

614
00:42:20,949 --> 00:42:30,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that restraint and renunciation, you must develop some standards that you're able to withhold the pain, withhold discomfort, without acting out of it right away.

615
00:42:30,273 --> 00:42:31,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sensuality is uncomfortable.

616
00:42:34,234 --> 00:42:37,055
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why... that's why it's so... it's so... so enchanting.

617
00:42:38,318 --> 00:42:46,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Because not just that you avoid the initial hit of discomfort of not getting the sensuality you want, but the underlying provides you with pleasure on top of it.

618
00:42:46,421 --> 00:42:47,581
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it feels like win-win.

619
00:42:47,921 --> 00:42:48,721
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't, isn't it?

620
00:42:49,021 --> 00:42:51,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Because fundamentally, you still act out of trying to avoid the pain.

621
00:42:52,523 --> 00:42:53,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill-will is uncomfortable.

622
00:42:54,763 --> 00:43:01,926
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not able to hold it without acting, without saying something, how can you then see your thoughts being entertained in regard to it?

623
00:43:02,046 --> 00:43:02,446
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

624
00:43:04,046 --> 00:43:04,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

625
00:43:05,747 --> 00:43:06,487
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to toughen up.

626
00:43:08,561 --> 00:43:12,143
[SPEAKER_00]: By toughening up, you develop confidence that you know you won't be acting out of it.

627
00:43:12,763 --> 00:43:19,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, you leave yourself no other choice, but to deal with the suffering that you have trapped and enclosed within yourself.

628
00:43:20,106 --> 00:43:31,111
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the first bits of the... the first talk you read from Ajahn Chah today, was that you must learn how to suffer, and then you'll know what to do, or how to do about suffering, how to go about suffering.

629
00:43:35,905 --> 00:43:38,347
[SPEAKER_00]: So, throughout the day, just keep reflecting, keep asking yourself.

630
00:43:38,748 --> 00:43:40,829
[SPEAKER_00]: You're engaged in certain actions right here, right now.

631
00:43:41,070 --> 00:43:42,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, why am I doing this?

632
00:43:42,711 --> 00:43:43,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is this rooted?

633
00:43:44,953 --> 00:43:45,433
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not sure.

634
00:43:45,493 --> 00:43:47,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, let that question then endure there a bit more.

635
00:43:49,277 --> 00:43:50,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this sensual?

636
00:43:50,798 --> 00:43:51,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill-will?

637
00:43:51,599 --> 00:43:52,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Carelessness?

638
00:43:52,319 --> 00:43:52,700
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

639
00:43:53,921 --> 00:43:56,062
[SPEAKER_00]: What would happen if I suddenly... this stops?

640
00:43:56,323 --> 00:43:58,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, whatever I'm doing gets taken away.

641
00:43:58,164 --> 00:43:59,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I be flustered?

642
00:43:59,545 --> 00:44:00,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I be affected?

643
00:44:01,107 --> 00:44:02,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean you have to go and destroy it.

644
00:44:03,458 --> 00:44:06,441
[SPEAKER_00]: That means you always have to be aware of what effect might have on you.

645
00:44:07,862 --> 00:44:15,649
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you get to learn from it.

646
00:44:18,011 --> 00:44:20,774
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll read a short verse.

647
00:44:21,034 --> 00:44:21,815
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's related.

648
00:44:22,115 --> 00:44:22,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh-huh.

649
00:44:22,816 --> 00:44:24,577
[SPEAKER_01]: In terms of striving.

650
00:44:24,597 --> 00:44:25,498
[SPEAKER_01]: You're saying toughen up.

651
00:44:26,279 --> 00:44:26,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

652
00:44:26,459 --> 00:44:27,360
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to toughen up.

653
00:44:28,222 --> 00:44:38,871
[SPEAKER_01]: It's from the Therigata, 3.5, by a disciple of the Buddha named Matanga, the son of Matanga.

654
00:44:40,612 --> 00:44:43,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So, his verse...

655
00:44:44,736 --> 00:44:49,300
[SPEAKER_01]: It's too cold, too hot, too late, they say.

656
00:44:50,221 --> 00:44:54,144
[SPEAKER_01]: When the young neglect their work like this, opportunities pass them by.

657
00:44:55,099 --> 00:45:04,161
[SPEAKER_01]: But one who considers hot and cold as nothing more than blades of grass, he does his manly duty and his happiness never fails.

658
00:45:04,781 --> 00:45:05,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

659
00:45:05,761 --> 00:45:12,943
[SPEAKER_01]: With my chest, with my chest I'll fuss aside the grasses, vines and creepers and foster seclusion.

660
00:45:14,543 --> 00:45:16,003
Yeah.

661
00:45:17,164 --> 00:45:18,964
[SPEAKER_00]: No, we spoke about it many times before.

662
00:45:21,264 --> 00:45:24,045
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, modern world, especially Western world,

663
00:45:24,509 --> 00:45:30,532
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think it's too different, but... yeah, everything, everything is wanted on easy terms.

664
00:45:30,572 --> 00:45:33,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is wanted on my own terms.

665
00:45:33,714 --> 00:45:37,816
[SPEAKER_00]: And then people think, oh, they had all the luck being born in the time of the Buddha.

666
00:45:38,417 --> 00:45:40,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there was a bit of luck, but...

667
00:45:40,718 --> 00:45:41,538
[SPEAKER_00]: They were never too hungry.

668
00:45:41,558 --> 00:45:43,759
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not, yeah, that's not why they were lucky.

669
00:45:44,180 --> 00:45:46,601
[SPEAKER_00]: They were lucky because the circumstances were harder.

670
00:45:47,634 --> 00:45:49,375
[SPEAKER_00]: were optimal, as the Buddha would say.

671
00:45:49,455 --> 00:45:54,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Not harder in a sense they were tortured, but harder in a sense they could have not be existentially spoiled.

672
00:45:56,798 --> 00:45:57,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Take things for granted.

673
00:45:57,979 --> 00:45:59,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality proliferated left and right.

674
00:46:00,080 --> 00:46:00,940
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just impossible.

675
00:46:02,161 --> 00:46:03,221
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they were lucky.

676
00:46:03,541 --> 00:46:05,062
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the time was optimal.

677
00:46:06,983 --> 00:46:16,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they were kept in check by circumstances, the mind could not have proliferated, which means it wasn't as polluted as the minds today are, on average.

678
00:46:18,546 --> 00:46:19,406
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the difference.

679
00:46:20,307 --> 00:46:27,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So, today, in order for people to understand, they need to do much more self-induced pollution that they've been cultivating throughout their life.

680
00:46:29,090 --> 00:46:32,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Through education, relationship, everything else, you just keep adding to it.

681
00:46:34,492 --> 00:46:35,313
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the difference.

682
00:46:36,373 --> 00:46:43,096
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could also see, like, just from the point of view of that, like, toughen up with mental toughness or being able to withhold the pain.

683
00:46:44,636 --> 00:46:46,837
[SPEAKER_00]: On average, how much softer we are today.

684
00:46:48,850 --> 00:46:49,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Much, much softer.

685
00:46:50,911 --> 00:46:56,514
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, back in the day, you know, like, you lose your whole family in a flood.

686
00:46:56,954 --> 00:46:58,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Six of your eight children die.

687
00:46:58,355 --> 00:47:02,017
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you go on.

688
00:47:03,377 --> 00:47:06,779
[SPEAKER_00]: But today, it's just like not being able to have a child.

689
00:47:07,579 --> 00:47:08,840
[SPEAKER_00]: You need 20 years of counseling.

690
00:47:11,181 --> 00:47:11,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not to have dinner.

691
00:47:12,562 --> 00:47:12,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

692
00:47:12,882 --> 00:47:13,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

693
00:47:13,282 --> 00:47:14,083
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't get enough food.

694
00:47:15,698 --> 00:47:17,119
[SPEAKER_01]: You get enough breakfast and lunch.

695
00:47:17,139 --> 00:47:17,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, yeah.

696
00:47:17,800 --> 00:47:20,763
[SPEAKER_00]: No, actually, nobody, like, in certain in the Western world, everybody gets enough food.

697
00:47:20,823 --> 00:47:27,369
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, well, most people get enough food, but it's more like, not as much as you used to, or something like that.

698
00:47:28,510 --> 00:47:29,691
[SPEAKER_00]: No, those are the little things.

699
00:47:29,711 --> 00:47:33,474
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, like, you start reflecting, like, why am I frightened in the prospect of this?

700
00:47:33,975 --> 00:47:40,581
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember when I, when I started reading the, the suttas and then the idea, okay, renunciation and precepts, and I was like, I want to do the fasting.

701
00:47:41,376 --> 00:47:43,277
[SPEAKER_00]: But no fasting even, like, I'll never eat or something.

702
00:47:43,317 --> 00:47:46,459
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, I'll skip a dinner today, and I'll get this, like, anxiety.

703
00:47:47,279 --> 00:47:49,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'll go to bed hungry.

704
00:47:49,160 --> 00:47:50,841
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I was only about 16, 17.

705
00:47:51,181 --> 00:47:55,064
[SPEAKER_00]: But still, I'm thinking, you're not gonna die.

706
00:47:55,124 --> 00:47:56,304
[SPEAKER_00]: You ate twice that day.

707
00:47:56,364 --> 00:47:56,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe even more.

708
00:47:56,985 --> 00:47:57,505
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't even remember.

709
00:47:57,985 --> 00:48:01,907
[SPEAKER_00]: I just decide to skip the habit of dinner.

710
00:48:03,128 --> 00:48:04,649
[SPEAKER_00]: And my mind was freaking out.

711
00:48:05,283 --> 00:48:10,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Fortunately, for me, with my own views at the time, I saw that as a challenge that I must overcome.

712
00:48:11,187 --> 00:48:12,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Many people would not necessarily think that.

713
00:48:12,808 --> 00:48:14,169
[SPEAKER_00]: They would think, okay, no, I'll just go and eat.

714
00:48:14,529 --> 00:48:15,589
[SPEAKER_00]: I shouldn't be doing this.

715
00:48:16,710 --> 00:48:34,061
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you will get so frightened just by not shoving enough... not shoving the same amount of food in your mouth, which is more than enough, even what you already ate, for sustenance, macronutrients, everything is already covered, yet you're still freaking out for not eating what you're used to.

716
00:48:35,072 --> 00:48:37,133
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean you're existentially spoiled.

717
00:48:37,634 --> 00:48:44,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the threshold of what you deem is necessary is far too inflated than what actually is necessary.

718
00:48:44,879 --> 00:48:53,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why, yes, circumstances simplification of many things in today modern life is... it's more likely going to result in good than in bad.

719
00:48:54,086 --> 00:49:05,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if they don't take it further, in terms of practice, dhamma and everything else, it's certainly gonna prevent depression and all sorts of modern ailments that kind of plague the Western world.

720
00:49:07,397 --> 00:49:09,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everything bothers you, because you're too soft.

721
00:49:10,759 --> 00:49:14,582
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the guidelines, the precepts, they're there.

722
00:49:15,003 --> 00:49:16,203
[SPEAKER_01]: You can find them easily.

723
00:49:16,464 --> 00:49:16,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely.

724
00:49:17,324 --> 00:49:18,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Just take those on.

725
00:49:18,484 --> 00:49:22,846
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's been around even after, you know, not necessarily like in Stoicism and all these other...

726
00:49:22,886 --> 00:49:23,526
[SPEAKER_00]: It was the same.

727
00:49:23,606 --> 00:49:30,329
[SPEAKER_00]: People realize that if you start to, like, practice the degree of self-restraint, you develop certain strength.

728
00:49:30,589 --> 00:49:44,615
[SPEAKER_00]: That the whole apathy, the word apathy from apatheia, the Greek, I think it's Greek, Latin, Greek, literally means indifferent, means equanimous, means even in regard to anything that comes your way.

729
00:49:46,466 --> 00:49:53,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, of course, there is the ultimate evenness of arahantship, but even the relative evenness is a good place to start or something to strive for.

730
00:49:54,709 --> 00:50:04,994
[SPEAKER_00]: There were those suttas where the Buddha described those two kingdoms and said why this kingdom will always win is because they sleep on wooden pillows, they bathe in cold water, they're like... Wake up before the sun.

731
00:50:05,054 --> 00:50:06,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Wake up before the sun.

732
00:50:06,795 --> 00:50:07,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

733
00:50:07,495 --> 00:50:12,537
[SPEAKER_00]: They're always vigilant, live on that kind of physical hardship to a degree.

734
00:50:13,038 --> 00:50:13,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Which keeps them sharp.

735
00:50:14,419 --> 00:50:14,559
[SPEAKER_00]: It's...

736
00:50:14,859 --> 00:50:20,984
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, Puthujana cannot necessarily decide or make myself wise or I prevent myself from proliferating.

737
00:50:21,505 --> 00:50:29,091
[SPEAKER_00]: But Puthujana can certainly recognize that circumstances can play the part in his mind proliferating towards sensuality or real will.

738
00:50:29,731 --> 00:50:33,014
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, he prevents the circumstances from spoiling him.

739
00:50:34,675 --> 00:50:37,177
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what those kingdoms, they would always win, do.

740
00:50:37,898 --> 00:50:39,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Those were the values that they cherished.

741
00:50:41,013 --> 00:50:50,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you put them in the environment of getting after the sun, bathing with soft, nice, scented, warm water, they will go the same way.

742
00:50:50,436 --> 00:50:51,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Human mind is the human mind.

743
00:50:52,077 --> 00:50:53,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Universal nature is the same.

744
00:50:54,337 --> 00:50:58,339
[SPEAKER_00]: But you recognize the circumstances, so you prevent yourself from ending up in that way.

745
00:50:59,639 --> 00:51:01,620
